<title>USA: U.S. Dec leading indicators +0.1 pct-Conference Bd.</title>
<headline>U.S. Dec leading indicators +0.1 pct-Conference Bd.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-02-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Conference Board index of leading, coincident and lagging indicators (1992=100) and percent changes from previous months, seasonally adjusted.</p>
<p>    -			Index---Dec   Nov   Oct   Sep   Aug</p>
<p> Leading Indicators    102.8   0.1   0.2  Unch   0.1   0.1</p>
<p> Coincident Indicators 114.1   0.4   0.4   0.1   0.2   0.3</p>
<p> Lagging Indicators    104.8   0.3  Unch  -0.1  -0.1  Unch</p>
<p>Following are components of the index of leading indicators with net contributions to monthly changes.</p>
<p>    -			    Dec    Nov    Oct   Sept    Aug</p>
<p> Average Workweek	   0.10   Unch   Unch   Unch   0.03</p>
<p> Initial Jobless Claims  -0.08  -0.01   0.01  -0.06   0.07</p>
<p> Consumer Goods Orders   -0.06  -0.04   0.02   0.02  -0.04</p>
<p> Slower Deliveries	  Unch   0.03   0.03  -0.09   0.05</p>
<p>    -			    Dec    Nov    Oct   Sept    Aug</p>
<p> Capital Goods Orders     Unch  -0.06   Unch   0.11  -0.09</p>
<p> Building Permits	   Unch   0.04  -0.03  -0.02  -0.03</p>
<p> Stock Prices		 0.02   0.10   0.08   0.04   0.06</p>
<p> Money Supply		 0.11   0.09  -0.01   0.02   0.06</p>
<p> Treasury Yield Curve     0.02  -0.08  -0.05   0.02  -0.01</p>
<p> Consumer Expectations   -0.03   0.05   Unch   0.04   0.01</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had expected December leading indicators to rise 0.1 percent.</p>
<p>During the six-month period through December, seven of the 10 leading indicators advanced, the Conference Board said. It said that in December six of the 10 leading indicators rose.</p>
</text>
